# Loadout session

This folder is a scratch session opened by `start-dotfiles-change`. {{ if $m.omalauncher }}You run in
this phone's terminal (omalauncher has no tmux), and you do the work
{{ else }}You run in the
`agent` window of a tmux session named after it, and you do the work
{{ end }}yourself, in the repos below. This session's settings already let you read
them and edit their worktrees, so nothing needs adding: you open what a
request needs, when it needs it. Work this way from the first message on;
nobody will ask for it by name.

## Where a request goes

| The request is about… | Repo | Where you edit |
|---|---|---|
| a new or changed loadout: a program, a script, a dotfile, a machine, a profile, `data` | `~/.local/share/chezmoi` | a feature worktree{{ if not $m.omalauncher }}, window `loadouts <feature>`{{ end }} |
| the loadout app itself: engine, CLI, home screen, its tests or release | `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout` | a feature worktree{{ if not $m.omalauncher }}, window `loadout <feature>`{{ end }} |
| loadout's user docs | `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout.wiki` | a feature worktree{{ if not $m.omalauncher }}, window `wiki <feature>`{{ end }} |
| a general-purpose agent skill | `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/skills` | a feature worktree{{ if not $m.omalauncher }}, window `skills <feature>`{{ end }} |
{{- if $m.omalauncher }}
| a widget, face, app, panel or key bar for this phone's launcher, its style or its home layout | `~/.config/omalauncher/widgets` | in place, no worktree (see Widgets and faces) |
{{- end }}
{{- if stat (joinPath .chezmoi.homeDir ".config/loadout/repo") }}

**This machine's loadout is a child repo of `.dotfiles`** (`LOADOUT_REPO`
points at it; see "This machine's repos" at the end). Its machine file, what
it installs and runs, and anything that names the work live in the child, not
in `.dotfiles`, which is public. A loadout change here goes to one of them, or
both:

- **`.dotfiles`**: what any machine could use and names nothing private: a
  program or script declared generically, a profile, a `Data` key, a hook a
  child's file plugs into.
- **The child**: this machine's own. Adding a program to this laptop is an
  edit of the child's machine file, even when the program is `.dotfiles`'.
- **Both**: a new hook and the file that uses it, a new `Data` key and the
  machine that sets it. Two worktrees and two commits, `.dotfiles` first: the
  child compiles against `.dotfiles`' main checkout, so its part compiles
  after `.dotfiles`' part has merged.
{{- end }}

Unsure which repo, or a request that spans two (an engine feature and its
wiki page, a feature and the `min-tool-version` bump in `.dotfiles`): say
which repos you'd touch and ask first. Each repo's part is its own
worktree and its own commit.

**A message starting with a repo and a colon goes there, no guessing:**
`dotfiles:`, `loadout:`, `wiki:`, `skills:`{{ if $m.omalauncher }}, `widgets:`{{ end }}. The
rest of the message is the task. `loadout:` is always the app; a new or
changed loadout is `dotfiles:`.{{ if stat $extra }} This machine has repos
of its own too, with their own prefixes: see "This machine's repos" at the
end.{{ end }}

## Starting work in a repo

1. **Read that repo's `AGENTS.md` before your first edit there**, and follow
   it for the rest of the session: it decides how features are made, tested
   and committed (in `.dotfiles`: commit only when asked, `--source` inside a
   worktree, never apply a whole worktree).
{{ if $m.omalauncher -}}
2. **A feature worktree**, with plain git: this phone has no workmux.
   ```bash
   git -C ~/.local/share/chezmoi worktree add ~/.local/share/chezmoi__worktrees/<feature> -b <feature>
   ```
   Name the feature short and say why the change is being made, e.g.
   `kitty-dashboard-key`, `finish-feature-rule`. That name is the branch and
   the worktree folder. Every repo's part of one feature uses the same name.
   The dotfiles' worktrees go beside the source, never inside it: anything
   inside `~/.local/share/chezmoi` is a chezmoi source entry. The others are
   bare repos, and theirs go in `all_worktrees/<feature>`, from their default
   branch:
   ```bash
   git -C ~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/<repo> worktree add all_worktrees/<feature> -b <feature> <default>
   ```
   The default branch is `master` for `loadout` and `wiki` (`loadout.wiki`),
   `main` for `skills`. `widgets:` requests get no worktree.
3. **Work there**: read and edit its files by their full paths, run its
   commands with `cd <worktree> && …`. Say which worktree you used.
{{ else -}}
2. **A feature worktree**, by the repo's own workmux config, without
   workmux's windows or agent (you are the agent):
   ```bash
   cd ~/.local/share/chezmoi && workmux add <feature> --headless
   ```
   Name the feature short and say why the change is being made, e.g.
   `kitty-dashboard-key`, `finish-feature-rule`. That name is the branch, the
   worktree folder, and the part of the window's name after its prefix (step
   3). Every repo's part of one feature uses the same name.
   It lands at `~/.local/share/chezmoi__worktrees/<feature>`. The others are
   bare repos: run it from their default worktree, and it lands beside it in
   `all_worktrees/<feature>`.
   For `loadout`, `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout/all_worktrees/master`.
   For `wiki`, `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout.wiki/all_worktrees/master`.
   For `skills`, `~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/skills/all_worktrees/main`.
3. **A window for the user**, a shell in that folder, in this session,
   running `nvim -c G` (fugitive's git status) so they can follow the
   changes; quitting nvim leaves the shell. Its
   name is the repo's prefix from the table, a space, then the feature's
   name: `loadouts kitty-dashboard-key`, or `wiki kitty-dashboard-key` for the
   wiki part of the same feature. The prefix tells the repos apart, and the
   feature name matches the worktree.
   ```bash
   tmux list-windows -F '#W'                                   # taken already?
   w=$(tmux new-window -d -P -F '#{window_id}' -n "<prefix> <feature>" -c ~/.local/share/chezmoi__worktrees/<feature>)
   tmux send-keys -t "$w" 'nvim -c G' Enter
   ```
4. **Work there**: read and edit its files by their full paths, run its
   commands with `cd <worktree> && …`. Say which worktree and window you
   used. Merging and removing a worktree follow the repo's `AGENTS.md`.
{{ end }}
## Finishing a feature

When the user asks you to finish a feature, do this for every repo and
worktree the feature touched in this session, in this order:

{{ if $m.omalauncher -}}
1. **Commit** everything in the worktree, in the repo's commit style and with
   its `AGENTS.md`'s rules. Launcher changes (`widgets:`) are in the dotfiles'
   main checkout already, on `master`: commit them there first, as their own
   commit, or the merge below can't move `master`. If anything is still
   uncommitted, warn the user and stop.
2. **Merge** into the repo's default branch (`master`, or `main` for `skills`),
   following the repo's `AGENTS.md`: rebase the feature onto it
   (`git -C <worktree> rebase <default>`), then fast-forward it from the main
   checkout (the default branch's worktree, for the bare repos) with
   `git merge --ff-only <feature>`. In `.dotfiles`, then `chezmoi diff` and
   `chezmoi apply` from the main checkout. If the worktree isn't merged, warn
   the user and stop.
3. **Push** the default branch and the feature branch. If anything is still
   unpushed, warn the user and stop.
4. **Clean up**, only once every touched worktree has passed steps 1 to 3:
   for a dotfiles worktree, first `dotfiles-worktree-check <worktree>` (what
   workmux's `pre_remove` runs on the desktops: files only the worktree
   manages that exist in `$HOME`), and if it lists any, ask the user before
   going on; then `git worktree remove <worktree>` and `git branch -d
   <feature>` in each repo. Last, `touch .claude/finished` in this session's
   folder and tell the user to quit claude (`/exit`): start-dotfiles-change
   removes the folder once claude exits.
{{ else -}}
1. **Check the tmux server's folder**: run `tmux-server-cwd`. A server keeps
   the folder it was started from for life, and once that folder is deleted
   tmux ignores `-c` and opens every new pane there. If it prints a path
   inside this session's folder (the folder holding this file) or inside a
   worktree the feature touched, or one ending in ` (deleted)`, warn the user
   and stop before step 2: merging removes the worktree, and cleaning up
   removes this folder. Only restarting the server fixes it, and that ends
   every session, so it's theirs to do; they finish the feature again after.
   Compare real paths (`realpath`), not the way they're spelled.
2. **Commit** everything in the worktree, in the repo's commit style and with
   its `AGENTS.md`'s rules. If anything is still uncommitted, warn the user
   and stop.
3. **Merge** into the repo's default branch (`master`, or `main` for `skills`),
   following the repo's `AGENTS.md`. In `.dotfiles` that is `workmux merge
   --cleanup` from the worktree, then `chezmoi diff` and `chezmoi apply` from
   the main checkout. If the worktree isn't merged, warn the user and stop.
4. **Push** the default branch and the feature branch. If anything is still
   unpushed, warn the user and stop.
5. **Clean up**, only once every touched worktree has passed steps 1 to 4:
   remove each worktree the way the repo's `AGENTS.md` says, then remove this
   session's folder and kill the tmux session this agent runs in, in one
   command and in that order (`rm -rf <folder> && tmux kill-session -t
   <session>`): killing the session ends this agent, so nothing after it runs.
{{ end }}
If any worktree fails a step, warn the user with the full list of what is
blocking (uncommitted, unpushed, unmerged), and remove nothing: the user may
still need those worktrees to fix it. Say which repos already merged or pushed
in the meantime.
{{- if $m.omalauncher }}

## Widgets and faces

`widgets:` requests are this phone's launcher: the files in
`~/.config/omalauncher/widgets` (widgets, faces, apps, panels, the key bar),
its style (`../style/style.toml`) and its home (`../home/layout.toml`).

- **Before the first edit**, read that folder's `README.md` (the OWL
  language, with `docs/` beside it; `examples/` has more to learn from) and
  its `AGENTS.md` (this phone's rules, which come before the launcher's own).
- **In place, no worktree**: they live on the phone, and saving one tracks it
  in the dotfiles' main checkout by itself (this session's hook runs
  `omalauncher-tracked`, as the widgets folder's own settings do).
- **After each save**, `owl-check <file>`: it prints the errors and the path
  of a PNG drawn with live data. Look at it to check the layout.
- **A new face** takes the empty page left of the leftmost one: faces sit
  left of page 1 at `page: 0`, `-1`, `-2`, … and `../home/layout.toml` lists
  the pages left to right.
- **Its packages** go in its `requires { pacman: [...] }`, installed with
  `loadout run omalauncher-requires`, never `pacman -S` by hand.
- **Committing** follows the dotfiles' `AGENTS.md`: only when the user asks,
  in the main checkout, on `master`.
{{- end }}

## The repos

- **`~/.local/share/chezmoi`** (josemiguelo/.dotfiles) — the one config
  repo. `configs/` is chezmoi's source (`.chezmoiroot`); the rest is what
  `loadout` reads{{ if stat (joinPath .chezmoi.homeDir ".config/loadout/repo") }}
  through this machine's child repo (`LOADOUT_REPO` points at the child, see
  "This machine's repos"){{ else }} (`LOADOUT_REPO` points here){{ end }}:
  Kotlin manifests (`loadout.kt`, `programs/`, `maintenance/`, `machines/`)
  that `loadout --repo <worktree> compile` turns into the committed
  `loadout.lock.json`, and `state/`. In a worktree, every loadout command
  takes `--repo <worktree>`: without it, `LOADOUT_REPO` picks the repo. A tool's loadout fragment sits beside its dotfiles as
  `.loadout.kt` + `.loadout/`. The
  main checkout stays on `master` and is what this machine applies; features
  live in `../chezmoi__worktrees/`. This file's source is
  `configs/private_dot_local/share/workflow-skills/NEW-LOADOUT-SESSION-AGENTS.md.tmpl`,
  rendered per machine.
- **`~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout`** (bare + worktree,
  `all_worktrees/<branch>`) — the `loadout` CLI (Kotlin/Native). A feature
  here often needs a `min-tool-version` bump in `.dotfiles` and a wiki
  update: separate repos, separate commits.
- **`~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/loadout.wiki`** (bare + worktree,
  `all_worktrees/<branch>`) — the user-facing wiki; its examples link into
  `.dotfiles` master.
- **`~/Repos/gh/josemiguelo/skills`** (bare + worktree) — general-purpose
  agent skills, linked into `~/.claude/skills/<name>` on every machine by
  `.dotfiles`' `skills-repo` script.
{{- if $m.omarchy }}
- **`~/Repos/gh/omacom/omarchy`** (plain clone, https, default branch
  `quattro`, releases tagged `vX.Y.Z`) — Omarchy's source, read-only: read
  release diffs, migrations and shell QML here, after `git fetch --tags
  origin`, with `git show <tag>:<path>` rather than checking tags out. Never
  make a temporary clone of Omarchy; if this one is missing, clone it here.
{{- end }}
{{- if $m.omalauncher }}
- **`~/.config/omalauncher/widgets`** — this phone's launcher files, in place
  (see Widgets and faces). `loadout`, `wiki` and `skills` are cloned here by
  the dotfiles' `bare-repos` and `skills-repo` scripts.
{{- end }}
- **A private, local-only repo** exists on at least one machine, outside
  this convention; it depends on `.dotfiles` and the skills repo. Leave it
  alone unless the user names it.
